.\" Manpage for eacct.
.TH man 1 "June 2026" "7.0" "eacct man page"
.SH NAME
eacct \- See a report of the last job executions reported by EAR's services.
.SH SYNOPSIS
eacct [options]

.SH DESCRIPTION

eacct is a command to see a jobs' energy accounting information.

.SH OPTIONS
.nf
.ft CW
eacct --help
Usage: eacct [options]

Options:
  -h, --help                       Displays this message.
  -v, --version                    Displays the current EAR version.
  -b, --verbose                    Enables verbose mode for debugging purposes.

Filters:
  -j, --jobs <jobid[.stepid]>      Filters by job ID and optional step ID.
                                   Also accepts comma-separated job IDs: jobid1,jobid2,...,jobidN.
  -u, --user <user>                Filters by username, only for authorized users.
                                   Non-privileged users can only retrieve their own jobs.
  -a, --app-name <app_id>          Filters by application ID. Default: all applications.
  -s, --start-time <YYYY-MM-DD>    Filters by start time. Default: no filter.
  -e, --end-time <YYYY-MM-DD>      Filters by end time. Default: no filter.
	-t, --energy-tag                 Filter by energy tag.
  -n, --limit <num|all>            Limits the number of jobs shown, from the most recent.
                                   Default: 5.

Output:
  -l, --long-apps                  Shows per-node metrics.
  -r, --loops                      Shows EAR runtime signatures.
  -o, --ext_loops                  Shows extended loop information. Use with --loops.
  -p, --eard-power-signatures      Prints EARD signatures even when EARL signatures are available.
  -F, --format <format>            Uses a custom format string. See the man page.
  -c, --csv <path|no_file>         Generates CSV output.
                                   Use 'no_file' to print CSV output to STDOUT. Default: off.
  -x, --events [job filter]        Shows the last EAR events.

.ft R
.fi

.SH Format characters

eacct by default shows a pre-selected set of columns. A format string may be specified (like "janf", which would show the Job-Step-AID, application name, node_id and average cpu frequency columns) to better select what data will be output by the program. As it stands, there are format characters available for all Signatures, Power_signatures, Jobs, Applications and Loop fields. Widely, they are categorised in two sets: Signatures (which includes power_signatures) and Jobs (which include Applications and Loops).

